ment  outgoing chancellor angela merkel had  been urging action at a meeting with  state premiers  with the current momentum we're running  into a very difficult situation  especially for people who work in  hospitals and particularly in intensive  care  we have always said that we don't want  to overburden our health system  we know that we can't prevent that  across the board right now but  fortunately we still have the capacity  to help in many places  but it is high time to act  other absolutely in the new covet 19 law  include a nationwide rule allowing  access to public transport only to those  who have been tested vaccinated or  recently recovered from the virus  with infection rates soaring more people  here want to see tougher measures  i think too little is being done i also  think the city hesitated for too long to  impose any restrictions  a general vaccine mandate more tests and  testing those who have been vaccinated  as well then things will get better  i think we have to introduce compulsory  vaccinations now  a general vaccine mandate isn't part of  the new law but a prominent health  expert believes the measures will make a  difference  restrictions that we decided today will  work if they are used  appropriately and if we really  use them to the fullest extent  the law has also been approved by the  second chamber representing the federal  states  the rules will now come to force as  early as next week  this was the first test for the new  constellation of federal and state  leaders  and one they couldn't afford to fail  well the new law includes several  measures let's take a closer look at  those now  anyone going to work or using public  transport needs to be vaccinated have  recently recovered from an infection or  they have to show a negative covid19  test here in germany that's known as the  3g rule  once a specific hospitalization rate is  reached those who are not vaccinated or  recovered will not be allowed to enter  bars restaurants large events that is  the so-called 2g rule and the measure  known as 2g plus that means those who  are vaccinated and recovered they will  still have to show a negative test  result to enter recreational venues now  there will also be a vaccine mandate for  certain professions like healthcare  workers or those who work in care homes  given that millions of germans reject  the vaccine this will likely be the most  controversial measure  let's bring in our correspondent nina  hasa who's following the latest on this  story for us hi nina good to see you so  we're talking about tighter covert  restrictions plans to boost the vaccine  uptake but we are in the middle of this  record-breaking fourth wave of the  pandemic so will this work  well angela merkel called it extremely  dramatic the current situation that  we're in uh so let me look at the  the fact that they're trying to boost  the vaccine uptake this morning the  outgoing health minister in spain held a  press conference like every friday again  now where he said in berlin that he's  noticing that people are beginning to  see the dramatic extent of the current  situation and he mentioned explicitly  the fact that gps and local doctors have  now ordered significantly more vaccine  doses than they did two weeks ago so  that means that people are approaching  their gps to get vaccinated for the  first time or even for the third time  for those booster vaccinations and the  hope is of course that more reluctant  people will get vaccinated now or else  they will risk losing pay at work for  example but there are still millions of  people who don't believe the science  that vaccines actually work and  regarding your first question will the  restrictions work well they will make  life a lot more difficult for  unvaccinated people but the plans are  coming very late and there are many  regions here now where there are no free  intensive care unit beds anymore and  those beds are occupied by mostly  unvaccinated covert patients and so the  health minister warned that germany  really is preparing for the collapse of  its health system let's take a listen  we are getting to a point now where we  will have to transfer covet patients not  just within the same region but to other  regions within germany  and quite possibly even abroad to  neighboring countries  the federal government and the states  are in close contact about that  sunina we see lawmakers making these  dramatic appeals to people is there  support for these measures across the  country  the debate in parliament on thursday was  intense and fractures they debated that  new law that is going to come into  effect now angela merkel's party the cdu  was against the passing of that law but  you wouldn't be wrong to think that  there was a certain degree of party  power play at work there in that debate  and in any case the conservatives then  didn't block the law today in the  bundesliga which is of course the second  chamber where the regions sit together  so the new restrictions are coming into  effect and as far as the general public  support for those restrictions is  concerned there is a lot of support  because there is a sense that  politicians essentially had taken their  eyes off the ball to a certain degree  over the summer people were busy with  the election campaign etc and that's  something that the president of the  bundestag actually accused the outgoing  government of  that they were being sluggish because of  the federal election so the majority of  germans now just want everybody to pull  together and get vaccinated so that this  fourth uh wave can actually be broken  dws nina hasa reporting for us good to  talk to you  well for more let's bring in dr uber  janssen he's the secretary general of  the german society for intense internal  intensive care and emergency medicines  dr janssen thanks for joining us on dw  the the numbers in germany are simply  soaring how worried are you  yeah  yeah  rebecca the numbers 